# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("A Narrow Walkway Is Mostly Edge, and the Base Has to Hold It",
     "<p>A driveway's base has a wide, continuous interior that holds its shape once compacted, but a 3- or 4-foot-wide walkway is mostly edge for its entire length, which means more of the compacted aggregate under a {city} walkway sits within a few inches of an unsupported side than it would under a wider slab. Loose material along that edge migrates or settles more easily than material in the middle of a wide pad, especially on sandy soils that do not hold a compacted edge well without some kind of restraint. We pay closer attention to edge compaction on a narrow walkway for that reason, not because the base spec itself is different from a wider slab's.</p>"),
    ("Finding Roots Before the Base Goes In, Not After",
     "<p>A front walk often runs closer to established landscaping than a driveway does, close enough that roots from a mature tree can already be under the planned path before anyone breaks ground. Checking for root paths during grading, and shifting a {city} walkway's line a foot or two where a trunk or major root makes that reasonable, costs far less than pouring over roots that lift the panel years later. Where the tree is large enough to be regulated, protection thresholds range from about 4 1/2 inches in diameter in Sarasota to 6 or 8 inches elsewhere in the service area, so confirming whether a specific tree is protected matters as much as the routing itself.</p>"),
    ("Stepping a Sloped Walkway Instead of Ramping It",
     "<p>On a sloped Central Florida lot, the ridge country west of Orlando especially, a straight-line walkway from the driveway to the front door sometimes drops more elevation than a continuous ramp can handle comfortably, which calls for the subgrade to be shaped in distinct level terraces rather than one even grade. Each step's landing gets its own compacted, leveled base rather than treating the whole run as a single sloped plane, since an uneven subgrade under a step shows up immediately as a tread that is not level. Getting that terracing right on a {city} lot starts with surveying the actual elevation change before deciding how many steps the walk needs.</p>"),
    ("A Front Walk Can Cross Two Different Soil Stories in One Run",
     "<p>A walkway running from the driveway to the front door is short, but it can still cross from open, sun-baked ground into the shade of an entry overhang or a tree canopy, conditions that hold moisture differently even over a run of twenty or thirty feet. On a {city} lot with flatwoods soils like Myakka or Smyrna, where the water table sits within 18 inches of the surface for part of the year, the shaded end of a short walk can stay wetter than the sunny end well after a storm has passed. Testing and compacting the base along the whole length, not just at the driveway end, catches that difference before it becomes an uneven slab.</p>"),
    ("Where Roof Runoff Lands on a Walkway That Hugs the House",
     "<p>A front walk that runs parallel to the house, rather than straight out from it, often sits exactly where runoff sheets off the roofline during a storm, a different drainage problem than a single downspout dumping water in one spot. Over a full rainy season, Orlando averages about 51.45 inches of rain a year and Sarasota-Bradenton about 49.05, that steady edge-wetting can undermine a compacted base along one whole side of a {city} walkway rather than at a single corner. Grading that strip to shed water away from the walk, or adding a gutter where there is not one, is worth doing before the base goes down, not after the slab is already cracking along that edge.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("Why the Sidewalk Section at a Driveway Is Often Thicker Than the Rest",
     "<p>Several jurisdictions across the service area hold the right-of-way sidewalk section where it crosses a driveway to a different, heavier spec than an ordinary front walk: Orlando's engineering standards call for at least 3,000 psi concrete and 6 inches of thickness at that crossing, and Orange and Seminole counties use the same 6-inch, non-steel-reinforced spec for the sidewalk section in the right-of-way. A plain front walk elsewhere on a {city} lot, by contrast, is commonly built closer to 4 inches thick. Confirming which spec applies to which section of a specific walkway, crossing versus open run, before the pour matters more than assuming one thickness covers the whole project.</p>"),
    ("A Curved Walkway Needs More Joints, Not Fewer",
     "<p>Joint-layout guidance calls for panels that stay close to square, with length no more than about one and a half times the width, a rule that is easy to follow on a straight run and harder to follow on a walkway that curves around a tree, a bed or a corner of the house. On a curved section of a {city} walkway, that usually means cutting joints more frequently than the standard 8-to-12-foot spacing used on a straight 4-inch slab, so each panel along the curve stays close to that near-square shape instead of tapering into a long wedge. Planning the joint layout around the curve at design time avoids an awkward sliver panel that cracks wherever it wants to.</p>"),
    ("Steps Are Their Own Structural Element, Not Just a Thicker Slab",
     "<p>A set of steps built into a sloped {city} walkway is not simply a thicker version of the flatwork around it: each riser and tread needs its own formwork, its own finish work for a non-slip surface, and often its own control joint separate from the joints in the level sections on either side. Treating steps as an extension of the surrounding slab, rather than a distinct piece of the job, is how a tread ends up slightly out of level with the riser below it. Building the steps as their own element, tied into the flat sections with a planned joint rather than a continuous pour, keeps each piece doing its own job correctly.</p>"),
    ("Why the Saw Comes Out on a Set Interval, Not Just by Feel",
     "<p>Walkway concrete gets cut on roughly the same joint-spacing logic as any flatwork, 24 to 36 times the slab thickness, which works out to about 8 to 12 feet on a standard 4-inch section, though at least one area jurisdiction's own paver-driveway sidewalk-crossing detail calls out saw cuts every 10 feet specifically. On a {city} walkway, we treat that kind of published local detail as the governing number where it applies, rather than defaulting to the general flatwork spacing, since the two can differ by a foot or two in either direction. A broom finish is standard across nearly all of a typical walkway, matching the texture used on driveways and patios for the same slip resistance.</p>"),
    ("Keeping Adjoining Panels Level at Every Joint",
     "<p>A control joint on a walkway does more than decide where concrete cracks; it is also where two panels meet, and if one side settles even slightly relative to the other, that joint becomes a lip instead of a flush line. Building a consistent, well-compacted base under every panel in a {city} walkway, and using dowels or a consistent subgrade treatment across each joint where appropriate, keeps adjoining sections moving together rather than independently as the ground underneath shifts with the seasons. That consistency matters more on a walkway than almost anywhere else in residential flatwork, since people's feet, not tires, are what cross every one of those joints.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("A Walkway Can Need Two Separate Permits at Once",
     "<p>Work on the stretch of a {city} walkway that sits in the public right-of-way, the part under the sidewalk or in the grassy strip next to the street, typically needs its own right-of-way permit, separate from whatever approval covers the rest of the walk on private property. That means a single front-walk project can run through two different review processes at the same time, each with its own timeline and inspection step, rather than one permit covering the whole run end to end. Confirming both pieces early, not assuming a private-property permit also covers the right-of-way section, keeps the project from stalling at the property line.</p>"),
    ("Adjusting the Layout the Day Roots Show Up",
     "<p>Even after grading identifies likely root paths, digging a {city} walkway's base sometimes turns up a root nobody expected, large enough that cutting through it risks the tree above it rather than just the concrete below. When that happens mid-dig, the crew has to decide on the spot whether to shift the walkway's line, bridge over the root with added base material, or pause and check whether the tree is protected under the local ordinance before doing either. That decision happens in the field, not back at the design table, which is why a crew familiar with the area's soil and tree rules matters as much on a walkway as the concrete work itself.</p>"),
    ("A Long, Narrow Pour Dries From Both Edges at Once",
     "<p>A walkway's shape works against it on a hot {city} pour day in a way a wider driveway slab does not: with almost the whole surface close to an edge, evaporation can pull moisture from both sides of a narrow section at the same time, raising the effective drying rate even when the air temperature and wind match conditions on a wider pour nearby. Starting early and watching the evaporation rate rather than just the clock matters as much here as on any other flatwork, but a narrow walkway gives a crew less margin for error before plastic shrinkage cracking shows up along both edges of the same panel.</p>"),
    ("Building Stepped Sections Takes Its Own Day",
     "<p>Forming and pouring a set of steps on a sloped {city} walkway is slower, panel for panel, than pouring the flat runs on either side of it, since each riser needs its own form, its own check for level, and its own finish pass before the next one goes in. That usually means the stepped section gets its own block of time in the schedule rather than finishing in the same pass as the open flatwork, even on a short walkway where the steps are only a few feet of the total run. Planning for that extra time up front keeps a stepped front walk from running into the same afternoon heat the flat sections were timed to avoid.</p>"),
    ("Forming a Curve Takes Longer Than Forming a Straight Run",
     "<p>A curved section of a {city} walkway needs flexible forming material bent to the actual curve, checked and rechecked before the pour, rather than the straight lumber forms that go up quickly along a driveway or a straight front walk. That extra forming time happens well before the concrete truck is ever scheduled, and rushing it is how a curve ends up slightly uneven or off the intended line once the forms come off. Budgeting more time for the forming stage of a curved walkway, rather than assuming it moves at the same pace as a straight run, keeps the schedule realistic from the start.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("A Driveway Crossing Built to the Wrong Spec Gets Rejected",
     "<p>Building the sidewalk section across a driveway to an ordinary front walk's 4-inch spec, when the local jurisdiction calls for a heavier crossing there, several areas require at least 3,000 psi concrete at 6 inches thick specifically where a sidewalk meets a driveway, is one of the more avoidable ways a {city} inspection fails. The fix usually means breaking out and repouring just that section, not the whole walkway, but it still costs a delay and a second round of concrete that a quick check against the local detail would have prevented. Confirming the crossing spec before forming that section, not after an inspector flags it, is the cheaper path.</p>"),
    ("A Lifted Panel Is a Trip Hazard, Not Just a Crack",
     "<p>Root heave on a driveway shows up as a cosmetic crack or a slight dip most drivers never notice, but the same heave on a {city} walkway creates an actual trip hazard where someone's foot catches an edge that was not there last year. Several local rules put maintenance responsibility for walkway work squarely on the property owner once it is built, which means a lifted panel is not just a repair question, it is a liability question too. Catching early tenting near a root before it becomes a full lip, and addressing it while the fix is still small, matters more on a walkway than almost anywhere else concrete gets poured.</p>"),
    ("A Walkway Without Enough Joints Cracks on Its Own Schedule",
     "<p>A long walkway poured without close enough joint spacing, or with panels that run long and narrow instead of close to square, does not crack less than a properly jointed one, it just cracks somewhere nobody planned for. That risk is sharper on a {city} walkway than on a wide driveway, since a narrow panel has less width to absorb stress before a diagonal crack runs clean across it rather than along a clean joint line. A walkway with this problem usually looks fine through its first year, then develops a crack that cuts across a panel at an angle, both weaker and more visible than the straight joint lines it should have followed.</p>"),
    ("Steps That Don't Account for Water Get Slippery Fast",
     "<p>A set of outdoor steps built without attention to drainage can pond a thin film of water on a tread after almost any rain, and that film turns a broom-finished step slippery in a way a flat section of the same walkway never is, since water on a slope behaves differently than water on level ground. On a shaded {city} lot, where a step sits under a tree canopy or close to the house, that tread can also stay damp long enough after a storm for algae to start building a film of its own. Grading each tread to shed water and choosing a texture that still grips when wet are both decisions made before the pour, not after someone slips.</p>"),
    ("An Uneven Joint Is a Hazard Before It's an Eyesore",
     "<p>A walkway panel that settles even a little relative to its neighbor creates a lip right at the joint, and on a path people walk every day in {city}, that lip is a trip risk long before it is noticeable as a cosmetic problem. The underlying cause is almost always uneven compaction or drainage under one panel and not the other, the same kind of soft spot that shows up as a cosmetic dip on a driveway, just with higher stakes on a surface meant for feet instead of tires. Catching the settlement early, while it is still a small offset, is far cheaper than releveling or replacing a panel after someone has already caught a toe on it.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Checking for Early Root Heave Along the Walk",
     "<p>A slight tenting or lift at one edge of a {city} walkway panel, especially near a mature tree, is worth noticing the first season it appears rather than waiting until it is an obvious trip hazard. Because tree protection thresholds differ by town, roughly 4 1/2 inches in diameter in Sarasota, 6 inches in Orlando and Winter Park, 8 inches in unincorporated Orange County, the right first move on a protected tree is checking the local ordinance before anyone reaches for a shovel or a saw. Catching heave early sometimes lets a contractor address just the affected panel without disturbing the root system at all, where waiting narrows the options considerably.</p>"),
    ("Sweeping the Right-of-Way Section Isn't Optional Upkeep",
     "<p>The part of a {city} walkway that crosses the public right-of-way is still the property owner's responsibility to maintain under several local rules, even though it sits on public land, which makes sweeping debris out of that section's joints more than a tidiness habit. Going into rainy season, a joint packed with sand and leaves at the driveway crossing cannot channel water the way an open one does, and that crossing section already carries a heavier load than the rest of the walk. A few minutes with a broom along that stretch each spring protects the section of the project most likely to need an inspector's sign-off again someday.</p>"),
    ("Watching a Step's Tread for Moss, Not Just Cracks",
     "<p>A shaded step on a {city} walkway, one that sits under a tree canopy or close to the house, holds moisture longer after rain than the flat sections on either side of it, which makes that tread a more likely spot for a thin film of algae or moss to build up over time. Checking a shaded step specifically, rather than relying on how the whole walkway looks from a distance, catches that film while a stiff brush and some water still handle it, before it gets slick enough to matter. It is a small, regular check worth doing more often on steps than on the level sections nearby.</p>"),
    ("Curved Sections Are Worth a Second Look Each Year",
     "<p>The forming work on a curved section of a {city} walkway is more complex than on a straight run, and that complexity carries into maintenance too: a curve's narrower, more varied panel shapes are more likely to show an early settlement sign than the long rectangular panels of a straight walk. Looking specifically at the curve, not just the walkway as a whole, when doing a seasonal check catches a soft spot while it is still a small dip rather than after it has become a visible lip at a joint. The straight sections of the same walk rarely need that same level of individual attention.</p>"),
    ("Telling a Normal Crack From a Panel That Needs Releveling",
     "<p>A hairline crack along a joint on a {city} walkway, especially one with a few years on it, is typically ordinary shrinkage, the same kind of cracking a driveway or patio develops over time. What is worth addressing is a panel that is visibly higher or lower than its neighbor at the joint, since that is a trip hazard rather than a cosmetic issue, and raising or releveling a sunken section is usually a smaller, more targeted repair than replacing the whole panel. Mudjacking-style releveling for a sidewalk-sized section tends to run as a modest line item compared with tearing out and repouring, worth knowing before assuming a lifted panel means a full replacement.</p>"),
  ],
}
